From f5c9997fd663f94888e4bd5be649fa7a8ed7fcb7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luca Bruni <lucbruni@amd.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 09:54:13 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] fix(rocr): wait for the dxg PM4 fence in the KMD, not
 with a 50 ms poll

On adapters without platform atomics, ComputeQueue::Process() completes a
packet's signal from the CPU after its submission's fence is reached.
Before #11344 this was a blocking D3DKMTWaitForSynchronizationObjectFromCpu
call. #11344 replaced it with a loop that reads the fence and sleeps 50 ms
between reads, to add a fault timeout. Every CPU-completed signal now costs
up to 50 ms (more with the default Windows timer resolution), so tests that
chain many small submissions run hundreds of times slower. On the gfx1036
Unit_hipGraph_BasicFunctional went from about 1 s to over 600 s, and
Unit_hipMultiStream_multimeDevice from 16 s to over 600 s.

Add WDDMDevice::CpuWaitTimeout(). On Windows it passes an event as
hAsyncEvent so the KMD signals it when the fence is reached, then waits
for the event with the fault timeout. Elsewhere it polls with a sleep that
starts at 10 us and grows to 1 ms. Process() uses it and keeps the 30 s
fault timeout and ROCR_DISABLE_WAIT_TIMEOUT behavior.
